WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.200 --> 00:00:08.090
It turns out that most prolines in
the T-Rex peptides identified by Asara

00:00:08.090 --> 00:00:13.630
were actually modified version of
prolines called hydroxyprolines.

00:00:13.630 --> 00:00:17.338
And in this segment,
we will talk about searches for

00:00:17.338 --> 00:00:20.670
post-translationally modified peptides.

00:00:20.670 --> 00:00:23.800
The central dogma doesn't mention it, but

00:00:23.800 --> 00:00:28.700
many proteins are subject to a large
number of post-translational

00:00:28.700 --> 00:00:33.280
modifications that are important for
cell-signaling and metabolic regulation.

00:00:34.280 --> 00:00:39.716
In fact, most proteins
are post-translationally modified and

00:00:39.716 --> 00:00:45.450
over 600 types of modifications
of amino acids are known today.

00:00:45.450 --> 00:00:50.740
Some of them are extremely important, such
as phosphorylation, shown on this slide.

00:00:52.040 --> 00:00:55.340
How does a modification
affect a peptide vector?

00:00:55.340 --> 00:01:01.290
If you think about this, modification is
simply changing the mass of the amino acid.

00:01:01.290 --> 00:01:04.700
If we limit ourselves to computational

00:01:06.570 --> 00:01:12.070
analysis of modification and
a modification of mass "delta".

00:01:12.070 --> 00:01:17.430
applied to an amino acid results
in adding data to its mass.

00:01:17.430 --> 00:01:21.380
For phosphorylation, for
example, delta = 80.

00:01:21.380 --> 00:01:28.100
For hydroxyproline in collagens,
it is equal to 16, and

00:01:28.100 --> 00:01:33.524
for a modification of lysine into allysin,
it is equal to -1.

00:01:35.070 --> 00:01:39.960
Therefore, modifications essentially
increase the number of letters

00:01:39.960 --> 00:01:43.930
in the amino acid alphabet and
complicate peptide identifications.

00:01:45.060 --> 00:01:51.490
In the beginning of this lecture, we looked
at the ideal spectrum of this peptide.

00:01:51.490 --> 00:01:55.580
Let's now look at how this
ideal spectrum changes

00:01:55.580 --> 00:01:58.630
if we introduce a modification
on this peptide.

00:01:58.630 --> 00:02:04.480
Here's this modification on C+16,
and this modification affects

00:02:06.040 --> 00:02:11.860
two prefix peptides and,
in fact, three suffix peptides.

00:02:11.860 --> 00:02:16.500
So, the ideal spectrum has
changed in this case.

00:02:16.500 --> 00:02:22.193
But please note that we don't know in
advance what is the mass of modifications,

00:02:22.193 --> 00:02:27.490
and we don't know on which amino
acid this modification is residing.

00:02:29.230 --> 00:02:33.580
Let's also take a look at how
a modification affects a peptide vector.

00:02:34.630 --> 00:02:40.280
In this particular example,
a modification of mass 3 on the i-th

00:02:40.280 --> 00:02:45.660
amino acids essentially amounts
to inserting a block of 3

00:02:45.660 --> 00:02:51.470
consecutive 0s before the i-th
occurrence of 1 in the peptide vector.

00:02:52.540 --> 00:02:56.110
This is for
the modification of positive mass.

00:02:56.110 --> 00:03:00.339
For a modification of negative mass,
-2 in this case,

00:03:02.220 --> 00:03:06.950
the modification corresponds to
deleting the block of 2 0s

00:03:06.950 --> 00:03:11.130
before the i-th occurrence
of 1 in the peptide.

00:03:12.650 --> 00:03:18.480
And after we figure out how
modifications affect the peptide vector,

00:03:18.480 --> 00:03:21.279
we are ready to formulate
the spectral alignment problem.

00:03:22.280 --> 00:03:28.730
Before we do this, let's define
the notion of Variants_k(Peptide)

00:03:28.730 --> 00:03:35.670
as the set of all modified variants of
Peptide with up to k modifications.

00:03:36.930 --> 00:03:41.900
We want to solve the spectral alignment
problem, which is: Given a peptide and

00:03:41.900 --> 00:03:46.440
a spectral vector,
find a modified variant of this peptide

00:03:46.440 --> 00:03:51.340
that maximizes the peptide-spectrum
score among all

00:03:51.340 --> 00:03:56.020
variants of the peptide with
up to k modifications.

00:03:57.560 --> 00:04:02.700
Of course, there is a very simple solution
of this problem: We simply check all

00:04:02.700 --> 00:04:07.720
possible peptides in the set
Variants_k(Peptide)

00:04:07.720 --> 00:04:12.020
and see which of them has the maximum score.

00:04:12.020 --> 00:04:17.500
But this will be too slow
because our more ambitious goal

00:04:17.500 --> 00:04:21.260
is to solve the following
modification search problem:

00:04:21.260 --> 00:04:25.830
Given a Spectrum and a Proteome,

00:04:25.830 --> 00:04:31.150
find a peptide of a maximum score
against this Spectrum among

00:04:31.150 --> 00:04:35.280
all modified peptides in the Proteome
with up to k modifications.

00:04:36.650 --> 00:04:41.890
Obviously, to efficiently solve the
modification search problem, we need to

00:04:41.890 --> 00:04:47.350
come up with a very fast algorithm for
solving the spectrum alignment problem.

00:04:49.010 --> 00:04:53.424
And, if we figure out how to search for
modified peptides,

00:04:53.424 --> 00:04:58.379
we will immediately be able to search for
mutated peptides as well

00:04:58.379 --> 00:05:03.427
because the mutation search problem
results from simply substituting

00:05:03.427 --> 00:05:08.500
the word modifications for
mutations, like shown in this slide.

